WEBVTT

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:09.279
Hello, this is Jenna Fisher and Angela Kinsey from the Office Ladies' Podcast in Los Angeles.
大家好，我是来自洛杉矶《办公室女士》播客的珍娜·费舍尔和安吉拉·金赛。

00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:14.380
And something we love about How We Made Your Mother is the amazing community of this show.
我们非常喜欢《老爸老妈浪漫史》的一点，就是这个节目拥有非常棒的粉丝群体。

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.780
The fans and people that write in, we think you guys are awesome.
那些写信来的粉丝和听众们，我们觉得你们太棒了。

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.679
And I love when Josh and Craig get philosophical.
而且我特别喜欢乔什和克雷格谈论哲学的时候。

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:39.042
I mean, I love all the behind the scenes stories, but my favorite is when Josh suddenly starts quoting Einstein or some ancient philosopher, and then he and Craig basically break down the meaning of love, or the power of friendship, or forgiveness, or you name it.
我是说，我喜欢所有幕后故事，但我最喜欢的是乔什突然开始引用爱因斯坦或某位古代哲学家的名言，然后他和克雷格基本上会剖析爱的意义、友谊的力量、宽恕，或者任何你能想到的主题。

00:00:39.042 --> 00:00:44.558
But I often find myself feeling a little lighter, a little more hopeful, after listening.
但听完之后，我常常感到内心轻松了一些，也多了一份希望。

00:00:44.558 --> 00:00:47.719
And that's a pretty special thing to be able to do.
能做到这一点真的很特别。

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:52.740
And that's why we're thrilled to be adding the show to our Office Ladies Network.
这就是为什么我们非常激动能将这个节目加入到我们的“办公室女士网络”中。

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.020
Welcome, guys!
欢迎你们，伙计们！

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.219
Oh, and you better have us as guests real soon.
哦，你们最好尽快邀请我们去做客。

00:00:57.219 --> 00:00:57.878
Uh-huh.
嗯哼。

00:00:57.878 --> 00:00:58.820
Get on it.
抓紧安排。

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.619
I'm alone.
我孤身一人。

00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:05.609
Be I won't be soon.
但我很快就不会是一个人了。

00:01:05.609 --> 00:01:08.760
You're New York City.
你是纽约市。

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.379
When I see you.
当我见到你时。

00:01:11.379 --> 00:01:15.569
Please permit me to tell you everything.
请允许我告诉你一切。

00:01:15.569 --> 00:01:19.180
You're New York City.
你是纽约市。

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.489
Well, here we are.
好了，我们开始吧。

00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:25.579
Welcome to the first episode of season two of How We Made Your Mother.
欢迎收听《老爸老妈浪漫史》第二季的第一集。

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:27.079
I am Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德诺。

00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:33.579
I played Ted Mosby on the TV show called How I Met Your Mother, which ran on CBS from 2005 to 2014.
我在2005年至2014年间于CBS播出的电视剧《老爸老妈浪漫史》中饰演泰德·莫斯比。

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:37.799
I am here with the co-creator of that show, Craig Thomas.
今天和我在一起的是该剧的联合主创克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:39.640
Hello, Craig, it's great to see you.
你好，克雷格，很高兴见到你。

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:47.319
Hello, Josh, very excited to be back here with you and the fans and our new wonderful friends over at the Office Ladies Network.
你好，乔什，很高兴能和你、粉丝们以及我们在“办公室女士网络”的新朋友们重聚于此。

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:52.760
Oh, well then, we sometimes call it, which is just to save us the energy of saying all those syllables.
哦，顺便说一下，我们有时会简称它，这样就不用费力去念那么多音节了。

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.040
We don't have that kind of time.
我们可没那么多时间。

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.400
We're very busy people.
我们是很忙的人。

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.239
Yeah, we got to get on with it.
是的，我们得抓紧时间了。

00:01:58.239 --> 00:01:59.079
Tell this story.
讲讲这个故事。

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:00.000
Yeah, tell the story.
没错，讲讲这个故事。

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:00.840
How did this happen?
这是怎么发生的呢？

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.000
Well, yeah, we're delighted to be working with Jenna and Angela and their team.
嗯，是的，我们很高兴能与珍娜、安吉拉以及她们的团队合作。

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:18.439
So the way this came about was, you know, we were a kind of lean and mean operation for season, me, Craig, Alec, Doug, Alex, just this very small team, and we were just doing this all on our own.
事情是这样的，你知道，在第一季时，我们是一个精简高效的团队，只有我、克雷格、亚历克、道格、亚历克斯，我们只是一个小团队，完全靠自己做这个节目。

00:02:18.439 --> 00:02:27.240
And I called Jenna Fisher, my old pal from back in the days, because we were on big hit TV shows around the same time, so we got to know each other.
然后我给老朋友珍娜·费舍尔打了电话，因为我们当时都在热门电视剧里演出，所以我们认识。

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:35.520
And I was reaching out, one, to ask her advice as a trailblazer in the rewatch podcast space.
我联系她，一是想请教她作为重温类播客先驱的建议。

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:53.719
If she had any advice for us, but also more specifically, I wanted to see if she would come on our podcast to talk about the strangeness of being a decade or so of your life, being thought of as someone you're not, being the whirlwind of being on a hit show, like all these things.
如果她有什么建议，但更具体地说，我想看看她是否愿意来我们的播客，聊聊那种在十年左右的时间里，被误认为是剧中角色，以及身处热门剧集旋风中的那种奇妙感觉。

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:58.819
And before we even got into it, she said, are you guys working with a podcast company?
结果我们还没开始聊，她就问：“你们有和播客公司合作吗？”

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:00.500
And I said, no.
我说：“没有。”

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:07.840
And she said, well, Angela and I have started our own podcast company called Office Ladies Network.
她说：“我和安吉拉创办了自己的播客公司，叫‘办公室女士网络’。”

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.659
And we would probably be delighted to have you on it.
“我们很乐意让你们加入。”

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:14.939
She actually said we would be sight unheard podcast on the scene.
她其实说的是，我们还没听过你们的播客，但已经决定要签下你们了。

00:03:14.939 --> 00:03:17.659
Like, how do you say that sounds on her?
等等，那句话怎么说来着？

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:18.659
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:25.883
She she just and then her and Angela gave a couple episodes of spin and immediately called us and said, we love this.
她和安吉拉听了几集之后，立刻打电话给我们说：“我们太喜欢这个节目了。”

00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:30.204
We'd love to pull you under the umbrella of the office ladies network.
“我们想把你们纳入‘办公室女士网络’的旗下。”

00:03:30.204 --> 00:03:33.600
We'd love to cross pollinate in any ways that we could.
“我们很乐意以各种方式进行跨界合作。”

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.019
And we're so delighted.
我们真的太高兴了。

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:35.813
We're so thrilled.
我们太激动了。

00:03:35.813 --> 00:03:37.180
Craig, what do you have to add?
克雷格，你有什么要补充的吗？

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.177
We're so honored.
我们感到非常荣幸。

00:03:38.177 --> 00:03:39.234
They would ask us.
她们竟然会邀请我们。

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:40.525
They're so good at it.
她们做得太好了。

00:03:40.525 --> 00:03:41.464
They're so good.
她们太棒了。

00:03:41.464 --> 00:03:42.403
They're so good.
她们真的太棒了。

00:03:42.403 --> 00:03:44.750
Jenna and Angela, they are trailblazers.
珍娜和安吉拉，她们是先驱。

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:50.208
They kind of paved the way for like a comedy rewatch podcast like ourselves to come along to.
她们为像我们这样的喜剧重温播客铺平了道路。

00:03:50.208 --> 00:03:55.960
And so we're grateful that they kind of and took us under their wing and we're their partners now.
所以我们很感激她们能把我们纳入麾下，现在我们是合作伙伴了。

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:56.800
Yeah, thank you guys.
是的，谢谢你们。

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:57.639
Thank you to Jenna.
谢谢珍娜。

00:03:57.639 --> 00:03:58.479
Thank you, Angela.
谢谢安吉拉。

00:03:58.479 --> 00:03:59.379
We're huge fans of the office.
我们是《办公室》的超级粉丝。

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:02.539
You will hear them on here and us on there.
你们会在这里听到她们的声音，也会在那里听到我们的声音。

00:04:02.539 --> 00:04:04.939
We really will kind of cross pollinate.
我们真的会进行跨界合作。

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:05.780
It's going to be great.
这一定会很棒。

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:06.620
We're very grateful.
我们非常感激。

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:14.219
I mean, it's kind of cool that it's when we were growing up, like, crossover episodes were, you know, like, Laverne and Shirley would show up on Happy Days, right?
说真的，这很酷，就像我们小时候看的那种交叉集，比如《拉文和雪莉》出现在《快乐日子》里，对吧？

00:04:14.219 --> 00:04:16.060
Yeah, it's like, it's the time on Earth.
是的，那是那个时代的特色。

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:16.899
I miss that.
我很怀念那个。

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:17.819
I miss that so much tradition.
我非常怀念那种传统。

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:21.019
And we didn't do that in our era.
我们那个时代没做过这种事。

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:22.500
So we're doing it now.
所以我们现在正在做。

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.540
with our rewatch podcasts.
通过我们的重温播客。

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.579
We're doing it now.
我们现在正在做。

00:04:25.579 --> 00:04:26.420
I love it.
我喜欢这样。

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:30.699
I love retroactively, we're doing it in this other medium since TV is too hard now.
我喜欢这种追溯性的做法，既然现在拍电视剧太难了，我们就换种媒介来做。

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:33.899
It's too hard to make TV shows now, so now we'll just do it on a podcast issue.
现在制作电视剧太难了，所以我们就在播客里实现它。

00:04:33.899 --> 00:04:38.660
Yeah, so thank you to Jenna and Angela and their team over at Office Ladies Network.
是的，所以感谢珍娜、安吉拉以及“办公室女士网络”的团队。

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:45.540
We're thrilled to be part of the family and we are putting a pause on the YouTube segment.
我们很高兴能成为这个大家庭的一员，我们要暂停YouTube视频版块了。

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.300
Craig, and I'll just say this upfront, Craig and I are aging aggressively.
克雷格，我先直说了，我和克雷格正在疯狂变老。

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.459
which is very aggressively each day.
每天都在疯狂变老。

00:04:52.459 --> 00:04:53.939
I had a lot of work done over the summer.
我夏天做了很多医美。

00:04:53.939 --> 00:04:54.779
I don't know if you're talking about it.
不知道你是不是在说这个。

00:04:54.779 --> 00:04:57.579
It has not settled yet.
还没完全恢复好。

00:04:57.579 --> 00:05:05.579
It didn't take, we cannot, we in good contents, we cannot show you Craig Thomas' face right now until his work settles.
效果没出来，为了节目质量，在克雷格·托马斯恢复好之前，我们不能展示他的脸。

00:05:05.579 --> 00:05:16.699
If you were watching the show over on YouTube, thank you, we loved having you go over there and we're still gonna have some video components to the show, but we're not gonna be on YouTube every week.
如果你一直在YouTube上观看节目，谢谢你，我们很喜欢你在那里观看，我们之后仍会有一些视频内容，但不会每周都在YouTube上更新了。

00:05:16.699 --> 00:05:20.399
Yeah, you'll see fun videos on our socials but it won't be the full episode on YouTube.
是的，你们会在我们的社交媒体上看到有趣的视频，但不会是完整的YouTube版节目。

00:05:20.399 --> 00:05:23.459
And you can monitor Craig's healing, which we're all looking for.
你们可以监督克雷格的恢复情况，我们都在期待。

00:05:23.459 --> 00:05:28.459
Yeah, no, I'll assume the bandages will come off about episode 11 or 12 that we're discussing.
是的，我估计到我们讨论第11或12集的时候，绷带就能拆了。

00:05:28.459 --> 00:05:34.620
It's pretty hard to look at right now, I gotta be honest, it's kind of, there's like a drainage pipe going from each eye.
说实话，现在看起来挺吓人的，每只眼睛下面都连着一根引流管。

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.540
Josh, I haven't had the work done yet.
乔什，我还没做呢。

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.819
Okay, what is going on?
好吧，到底怎么回事？

00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:41.899
It's coming up, it's next week, this is horrible.
快了，就在下周，这太可怕了。

00:05:41.899 --> 00:05:42.980
Why don't we get to it, Craig?
我们开始吧，克雷格？

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.459
Why don't we launch this season two?
我们开始第二季吧？

00:05:45.459 --> 00:05:48.300
Turning now to our trusty producer, Alec Lev.
现在转向我们可靠的制作人亚历克·莱夫。

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.459
Alec, tell us the name of this episode and when it originally aired.
亚历克，告诉我们这一集的标题以及它最初播出的时间。

00:05:52.459 --> 00:05:53.620
Absolutely, and hi guys.
没问题，大家好。

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:54.459
Welcome back.
欢迎回来。

00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:55.300
Hi, Alec.
你好，亚历克。

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.779
Exciting, and I'm very excited also about this Office Ladies Network.
太令人兴奋了，我也对这个“办公室女士网络”（Office Ladies Network）感到非常兴奋。

00:05:58.779 --> 00:06:06.019
Jen and Angela have already been amazing to work with, thanks to them from me also, and Colin, who is part of their team as well.
与珍（Jen）和安吉拉（Angela）合作一直都很棒，我也要感谢她们，还有她们团队中的科林（Colin）。

00:06:06.019 --> 00:06:08.060
The episode is called Where Were We?
这一集的名字叫《我们刚才说到哪了？》（Where Were We?）。

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:14.579
It originally aired on September 18th, 2006, written by Carter Bays and Craig Thomas.
它最初于2006年9月18日播出，由卡特·贝斯（Carter Bays）和克雷格·托马斯（Craig Thomas）编剧。

00:06:14.579 --> 00:06:15.420
Where were we?
我们刚才说到哪了？

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.540
It's a great question to ask.
这是一个很好的问题。

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:18.980
We, uh, we had a title.
我们，呃，我们有个标题。

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:23.339
We wrapped up a season one and, and we, I haven't seen you for years.
我们结束了第一季，然后，我好几年没见你了。

00:06:23.339 --> 00:06:27.180
And by years, I mean, like six weeks, maybe six weeks.
说到“好几年”，我是指大概六周，也许六周吧。

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.259
It's been the smallest summer hiatus I've ever had.
这是我经历过最短的暑期停播期。

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:30.420
It isn't brief hiatus.
这不是那种短暂的停播。

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.620
It's not like the real, the real hymnium hiatus several months.
不像那种真正的、真正的几个月的漫长停播。

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.500
I would, I would not see you for a while.
我以前，我以前会有一段时间见不到你。

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.379
You would go off on crazy adventures.
你会去进行疯狂的冒险。

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.939
The writers would come back way before the actors.
编剧们会比演员们早回来很多。

00:06:40.259 --> 00:06:42.220
We worked for like 16 weeks before you got there.
在你们回来之前，我们大概已经工作了16周。

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.620
All the actors would come in looking tan and beautiful and be like, how was your guy's summer?
所有的演员回来时都晒得黝黑、光彩照人，还会问：“你们的暑假过得怎么样？”

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.819
we'd be like, we're haggard and gray.
我们会说：“我们憔悴又灰暗。”

00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:49.579
We haven't seen the sun.
我们都没见过太阳。

00:06:49.579 --> 00:06:51.259
Don't ask us how our summer was.
别问我们暑假过得怎么样。

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:55.060
But yeah, no, this was a quick turnaround.
但确实，不，这次的周期很快。

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:56.699
So it's fun to jump right back in.
所以能立刻投入工作很有趣。

00:06:56.699 --> 00:07:04.699
I love the meta title of where we, that was the feeling in trying to break this episode in the writer's room of like, how do we pick this up?
我喜欢“我们刚才说到哪了”这个元标题，这正是我们在编剧室构思这一集时的感觉，就像是，我们该如何衔接剧情呢？

00:07:04.699 --> 00:07:09.779
And here's my first thought, Josh, when I started watching this one, which I did last night and I loved it.
乔什（Josh），这是我开始看这一集时的第一个想法，我昨晚看了，我很喜欢。

00:07:09.779 --> 00:07:12.980
I hadn't seen this in years and years and years and years.
我已经好多年好多年没看过这一集了。

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.379
And the pressure coming in his episode was immense.
而且这一集带来的压力非常巨大。

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:29.019
We had been advised, slash scolded, to be less serialized, but in between the two season, season one and season two, we did not agree with that advice and we didn't really take it.
我们曾被建议，或者说被训斥，要减少连续剧的成分，但在第一季和第二季之间，我们并不认同那个建议，也没有真正采纳它。

00:07:29.019 --> 00:07:38.259
But we really wanted to come in and kill it to show that we could do this kind of serialized, emotional comedy show on CBS and have it find an audience, but we weren't there yet.
但我们真的很想一鸣惊人，证明我们可以在哥伦比亚广播公司（CBS）制作这种连续性的、情感丰富的喜剧节目，并找到观众，但我们当时还没做到。

00:07:38.259 --> 00:07:40.540
And so we really felt like we're singing for our supper.
所以我们真的感觉像是在为生计而战。

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:49.980
The thing the first thought I had was, It's so funny that we had to speak to the summer passing to still stay in season for when the TV show was airing.
我产生的第一个想法是，为了让电视节目在播出时仍处于当季，我们必须交代暑假的流逝，这太搞笑了。

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.899
You were just talking about crossover episodes in like 70s and 80s TV.
你刚才还在谈论70年代和80年代电视节目中的交叉集。

00:07:53.899 --> 00:08:00.579
Another seemingly now vestigial thing is this idea that you have to be in the season where you're airing, right?
另一个现在看起来已经过时的东西，就是这种“你必须在播出季节内”的想法，对吧？

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:02.180
On streaming, who cares?
在流媒体上，谁在乎呢？

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:03.019
Who cares?
谁在乎？

00:08:03.019 --> 00:08:03.860
Who cares?
谁在乎？

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.779
Why is it winter and andor?
为什么《安多》（Andor）里是冬天？

00:08:05.779 --> 00:08:09.100
It's like it's summer while I'm watching it here in the real world.
我在现实世界里看的时候明明是夏天。

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.220
I'm turning this off.
我要关掉它了。

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:20.660
Yes, but we, this is implausible now, but we, there was this kind of common understanding and we definitely didn't feel ready to buck that conventional wisdom.
是的，但我们，这在现在看来不可思议，但我们当时确实有一种共识，而且我们绝对没准备好去挑战那种传统观念。

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:33.379
You've gotta come in basically in the season so you can catch up to your Thanksgiving episode and your Halloween episode and your Christmas episode, all that stuff and you can be sort of in sync with where the viewer is watching it in time.
你基本上必须在当季播出，这样你才能赶上感恩节那一集、万圣节那一集和圣诞节那一集，所有这些东西，你才能与观众观看的时间保持同步。

00:08:33.379 --> 00:08:39.820
And that was what dictated the storytelling in this episode showing the whole summer passing and kind of catching up to ourselves.
这就是决定这一集叙事的原因，展示整个暑假的流逝，并以此赶上我们的进度。

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.899
Here we are again in September in New York City.
我们又回到了九月的纽约市。

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:47.019
I was just laughing at the idea that shows just don't have to do that anymore.
我刚才还在笑，因为现在的节目根本不需要那样做了。

00:08:47.019 --> 00:08:50.179
Well, let's just bring the listener up to speed.
好吧，让我们先让听众了解一下情况。

00:08:50.179 --> 00:08:53.820
So where we left our heroes, right?
我们上次把英雄们留在哪了？

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:58.820
So Ted makes this bold move to Robin in the rain.
泰德（Ted）在雨中对罗宾（Robin）采取了大胆的行动。

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:04.460
He shows up, they have this grand romantic cinematic kiss.
他出现了，他们有了一个宏大、浪漫、电影般的吻。

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:05.325
cinematic kiss.
电影般的吻。

00:09:05.325 --> 00:09:18.882
He returns home to his Upper West Side apartment from Brooklyn, which is a longer trip than people realize we've already established the geography is implausible that a woman in Brooklyn would hang out that much of the Upper West Side.
他从布鲁克林回到上西区的公寓，这比人们想象的要远得多，我们已经确定了地理位置是不合理的，一个住在布鲁克林的女人不可能经常在上西区闲逛。

00:09:18.882 --> 00:09:29.324
But he gets back to the Upper West Side and he's delighted he can't wait to get home and tell Marshall and Lily and Marshall is sitting out on the stoop holding the engagement ring.
但他回到了上西区，他很高兴，迫不及待地想回家告诉马歇尔（Marshall）和莉莉（Lily），而马歇尔正坐在台阶上拿着订婚戒指。

00:09:29.324 --> 00:09:30.940
Lily is gone and devastated.
莉莉走了，他心碎了。

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:37.659
So Ted has to pivot and sit down and sit with his best friend as they get pelted with rain.
所以泰德不得不转变情绪，坐下来陪他最好的朋友，任由雨水拍打在他们身上。

00:09:37.659 --> 00:09:42.460
One of my favorite moments in the whole series is that you putting your arm around him on those steps.
整个系列中我最喜欢的时刻之一，就是你把手臂搭在他肩上坐在台阶上的那一幕。

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.820
And that was the block party song, right?
那是街区派对乐队（Bloc Party）的歌，对吧？

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:44.820
Yeah.
是的。

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:49.700
This Modern Love by Block Party, one of my favorite needle drops, as they say, in the whole show.
街区派对乐队的《This Modern Love》，这是整部剧中我最喜欢的插曲之一。

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:56.940
So that's where we end season one, which I'm sure CBS was delighted by that happy Waltz to the sunset ending.
这就是我们第一季的结尾，我相信CBS对那个“华尔兹走向日落”的快乐结局感到非常高兴。

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.940
Tune in for more sadness in September.
敬请期待九月份更多的悲伤。

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:11.549
And I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小诗，送你在纽约市。

00:10:11.549 --> 00:10:13.149
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:19.629
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼（Alex Goldman），《Hyper-Fixed》播客的主持人。

00:10:19.629 --> 00:10:23.350
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并尝试为他们解决。

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:26.309
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如，“我30岁了，但我不敢在纽约开车”。

00:10:26.309 --> 00:10:29.990
Or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，“为什么我在倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？”

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:32.549
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决非汽车相关的问题。

00:10:32.549 --> 00:10:38.700
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有一个问题，我们不仅会解决它，还会揭露导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.220
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《Hyper-Fixed》播客。

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.740
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixedPod.com.
在任何你收听播客的地方或访问HyperFixedPod.com即可找到。

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:59.240
Facts the show.
事实就是这样。

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:00.960
So then where were we?
那么，我们刚才说到哪了？

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:16.720
We popped back into this episode and I think it's one thing that I told Jordana that was really funny as I was watching, I was like, so, so we're filming the presumably an hour or two after they get inside, Robin has come over, or maybe it's later that morning, right?
我们回到了这一集，我想我告诉乔丹娜（Jordana）的一件事真的很有趣，我在看的时候心想，所以，我们拍摄的时间大概是他们进屋后的一两个小时，罗宾过来了，或者也许是那天晚些时候，对吧？

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.200
No, yes, that's definitely.
不，是的，那绝对是。

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:20.659
We're still wet from the rain, right?
我们身上还湿着雨水，对吧？

00:11:20.659 --> 00:11:31.399
You can see that we've, we've dried off a little, but there's four months between the end of that scene and then, and I just remember, you know, the, the props or I think it was really wardrobe.
你可以看到我们已经稍微干了一些，但那场戏的结尾和现在之间隔了四个月，我只记得，你知道，道具，或者我觉得其实是服装组。

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:44.105
They come through with spray bottles, yeah, and they're trying to get you wet, you know, keep you kind of looking plausibly wet, but it's such a funny time jump, especially because most TV and film is shot out of order.
他们拿着喷雾瓶走过来，是的，他们试图让你湿透，你知道，让你看起来还算合理地湿着，但这真是一个有趣的跳跃，尤其是因为大多数电视和电影都是乱序拍摄的。

00:11:44.105 --> 00:12:00.233
So you have to really know the script well enough to keep that order in your head as you're going and know where your character is emotionally, because you could shoot the most emotionally impactful scene in the first couple of days, and then you're shooting the previous stuff.
所以你必须非常熟悉剧本，才能在拍摄时保持那种顺序，并知道你的角色在情感上处于什么位置，因为你可能在头几天就拍了情感冲击力最强的那场戏，然后才去拍之前的内容。

00:12:00.233 --> 00:12:00.988
So it's wild.
所以这太疯狂了。

00:12:00.988 --> 00:12:07.659
But anyway, just give us a quick armchair summary, Craig, of what happens in season two episode one, where were we?
但不管怎样，克雷格，给我们一个简短的总结，关于第二季第一集《我们刚才说到哪了？》发生了什么。

00:12:07.659 --> 00:12:13.080
Yeah, I remember in the writer's room feeling all this pressure until we realized two things.
是的，我记得在编剧室里感到压力很大，直到我们意识到两件事。

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:20.792
One, we're going to track the entire summer and show Marshall's descent into madness through the summer and catch up to where we are.
第一，我们要追踪整个夏天，展示马歇尔在夏天陷入疯狂的过程，并赶上我们现在的进度。

00:12:20.792 --> 00:12:23.053
That was the idea one that unlocked it.
那是第一个解开困局的想法。

00:12:23.053 --> 00:12:30.418
And idea two was this credit card idea where Marshall gets a hold of and midway through the episode of Lily's credit card bill.
第二个想法是关于信用卡的，马歇尔在这一集中途拿到了莉莉的信用卡账单。

00:12:30.418 --> 00:12:35.520
It's sent to him and the idea of just these pieces of evidence of what she's been up to.
账单寄给了他，这些证据碎片展示了她一直在做什么。

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:39.347
And that's going to sort of steer the second half of that episode.
这将引导这一集的后半部分。

00:12:39.347 --> 00:12:45.958
So that's basically this is showing the summer of these two roommates, one of whom is the happiest he's ever been.
所以这基本上展示了这两个室友的夏天，其中一个是他有生以来最快乐的。

00:12:45.958 --> 00:12:50.191
He got Robin and one of whom is the absolute saddest that he's ever been.
他得到了罗宾，而另一个是他有生以来最悲伤的。

00:12:50.191 --> 00:12:52.626
And that was just a compelling idea to us.
这对我们来说是一个引人入胜的想法。

00:12:52.626 --> 00:13:05.210
You guys had so much fun I could feel how much fun you had in playing with that idea that Ted is kind of euphoric but it's a funny thing when your friend is down you almost have to like put on your sad face with that.
你们玩得太开心了，我能感觉到你们在处理这个想法时有多享受，泰德处于一种欣快状态，但当你的朋友情绪低落时，你几乎不得不表现出悲伤的样子，这很有趣。

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:16.169
I mean it's a kind thing to be empathetic and be like buddy I know you know and also it's hard to not be able to share your joy with your best friend of what's happening with you, you know?
我的意思是，表现出同理心并说“伙计，我懂的”是一种善良，而且无法与你最好的朋友分享你身上发生的快乐，这也很难，你知道吗？

00:13:16.169 --> 00:13:33.623
I mean, the universe or whatever, the narrative storyteller who's writing this story, it's like, it never kind of, there's always this kind of, I've noticed like a kind of friction between where you are and where your friends might be or even your partners, and everyone's not always on the same page.
我的意思是，宇宙，或者任何编写这个故事的叙事者，我注意到你所处的位置和你朋友甚至伴侣所处的位置之间总有一种摩擦，每个人并不总是在同一个频道上。

00:13:33.623 --> 00:13:39.016
And that's something you have to negotiate with friendship and with just living in the world.
这是你在友谊中以及在世界上生活时必须去协调的事情。

00:13:39.016 --> 00:14:12.532
I loved what a good friend Ted was in that initial moment where Robin comes in who and she's going to say something about her and Ted getting the other and he's like no there's only one thing that happened in the news last night there's no other news stories here right and I loved Ted for that and I think it really earned Ted's later moment you see Ted really tending to Marshall's well-being the whole summer and it felt very earned later in the episode when Ted does kind of say enough stop acting this way yeah this isn't you you'll never get her back or move on like this.
我喜欢泰德在最初那一刻表现出的友谊，当时罗宾进来，她想说关于她和泰德的事，而他说“不，昨晚新闻里只发生了一件事，这里没有其他新闻了，对吧”，我因为这一点很喜欢泰德，我认为这确实为泰德后来的表现做了铺垫，你看到泰德整个夏天都在照顾马歇尔的健康，当泰德后来确实说“够了，别再这样做了，这不是你，你永远追不回她了，继续前进吧”时，感觉非常合理。

00:14:12.532 --> 00:14:16.301
And I, that's such a, we'll get there, but that, I love that arc.
而且，那是一个，我们以后会讲到，但我喜欢那个弧线。

00:14:16.301 --> 00:14:28.130
That was Ted's arc in the episode to start off as a caretaker and then becomes the one who kind of really tries to shake him out of it in the end in a way that is almost a little too much, but it's great.
那是泰德在这一集里的弧线，从一个照顾者开始，最后变成那个试图让他清醒过来的人，虽然方式有点过火，但很棒。

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:32.480
And that, that idea, we've all been on, I think both ends of that equation.
而且那个想法，我想我们都经历过那个等式的两端。

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:36.215
We've been the miserable guy that's being scraped off the floor by our friends.
我们曾是那个被朋友从地板上扶起来的悲惨家伙。

00:14:36.215 --> 00:14:41.181
And we've been the super happy guy who's like, I can't be this happy around this person I love right now.
我们也曾是那个超级快乐的家伙，心想“我现在在这个我爱的人面前不能表现得这么快乐”。

00:14:41.181 --> 00:14:42.600
I have to hide this happiness.
我必须隐藏这种快乐。

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:57.480
You know, there's an interesting thing that is like a real friendship dilemma that you guys were mining, I think, in this episode, which is one is like, I'm going to sign off on my friend's version of reality.
你知道，有一件有趣的事情，就像你们在这一集中挖掘的一个真正的友谊困境，那就是，我要认可我朋友的现实版本。

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:02.519
Like I'm going to say, yes, he did you wrong or she did you wrong or this is horrible or you're the victim here.
就像我会说，是的，他亏待了你，或者她亏待了你，或者这太可怕了，或者你是这里的受害者。

00:15:02.519 --> 00:15:13.679
Like whatever their idea is for a long time as your friend, you're like, yes, you have a right to be feeling what you're feeling and I am with you, and I hate them for you and with you.
无论他们有什么想法，作为朋友，你陪伴了很久，你会说，是的，你有权感受你现在的感受，我与你同在，我为你、也和你一起讨厌他们。

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.093
But then at a certain point, the better friend act is to say, enough.
但到了某个节点，更好的朋友做法是说，够了。

00:15:19.093 --> 00:15:23.664
I have a very dear friend who was really stuck around some stuff.
我有一位非常亲密的朋友，他当时在某些事情上真的很钻牛角尖。

00:15:23.664 --> 00:15:25.845
I don't want to go too into it.
我不想说得太细。

00:15:25.845 --> 00:15:35.480
But one of our other very dear friends, these are longtime friends, he really risked their friendship by saying, you've got to stop this.
但我们另一位非常亲密的朋友——他们都是多年的老友——他冒着破坏友谊的风险说，你必须停止这样了。

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:39.489
You've got to forgive this person, you've got to move on.
你必须原谅这个人，你必须向前看。

00:15:39.489 --> 00:15:43.358
And I really salute this other friend for risking that.
我真的很敬佩这位朋友敢于冒这个险。

00:15:43.358 --> 00:15:53.415
It can be very hard sometimes to speak capital T-Truth to a friend who's really got their stories set and to say like this is only hurting you.
有时候，对一个已经认定了自己故事的朋友说出“真相”（大写的T），并告诉他这只会伤害你自己，是非常困难的。

00:15:53.415 --> 00:15:58.480
Yeah, I think Ted was the best friendship Ted exhibited in that episode.
是的，我认为泰德在那一集中展现了最好的友谊。

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:00.764
It was when he was when he yelled at Marshall.
就是当他冲马歇尔大喊大叫的时候。

00:16:00.764 --> 00:16:01.757
I think it was time.
我觉得是时候了。

00:16:01.757 --> 00:16:07.120
That was the power of that that structure of seeing the whole summer best, day 56, day 67, blah, blah, blah.
那种结构的力量在于看到整个夏天，第56天，第67天，等等等等。

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:08.799
It's a long fucking time.
那可是他妈的漫长的一段时间。

00:16:08.799 --> 00:16:10.519
It's the whole summer, it's September now.
那是整个夏天，现在已经是九月了。

00:16:10.519 --> 00:16:16.320
And it's like, it's almost like Ted is saying in this meta way, like, we've still gotta be characters in this show.
这就像泰德在以一种元叙事的方式说，我们还得继续做这部剧里的角色。

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.080
We've still gotta, we've got this show to do.
我们还得继续，我们还有这部剧要拍。

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.519
People are watching it.
人们正在看呢。

00:16:20.519 --> 00:16:23.679
Also, it's like, narrative is about action.
而且，叙事就是关于行动的。

00:16:23.679 --> 00:16:26.000
You're just being a big baby on the floor.
你只是像个大婴儿一样瘫在地板上。

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.039
We've gotta do something, we've gotta take some action.
我们得做点什么，我们得采取行动。

00:16:28.039 --> 00:16:29.120
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊，是啊。

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:41.320
I just realized there's a very meta thing at the beginning where Marshall's gonna reach the phone to call Lily and doesn't Ted say I will punch you in the face and he says you're a good friend Ted.
我刚意识到开头有一个非常元叙事的地方，马歇尔伸手去拿电话要打给莉莉，泰德不是说“我会揍你的脸”吗？然后他说“你是个好朋友，泰德”。

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:42.320
Yeah.
是的。

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:47.679
And like he ends up actually kind of punching him in the face and it's him being a good friend like later.
最后他真的揍了他的脸，这反而是他后来作为好朋友的表现。

00:16:47.679 --> 00:16:48.519
Yeah.
是的。

00:16:48.519 --> 00:16:52.279
That's kind of like the synopsis of what happens in the episode, right?
这基本上就是那一集剧情的梗概，对吧？

00:16:52.279 --> 00:16:53.360
Yeah, it is.
是的，没错。

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:54.360
That's right.
就是这样。

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:56.679
He needs that punch in the end.
他最后确实需要那一拳。

00:16:56.679 --> 00:16:57.679
He needed that.
他需要那一拳。

00:16:57.679 --> 00:16:59.799
He needed to be shaken out of this.
他需要从这种状态中被震醒。

00:16:59.799 --> 00:17:01.240
Yeah, I remember the writer's room.
是的，我记得编剧室。

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:03.860
everyone had their breakup story, right?
每个人都有自己的分手故事，对吧？

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:13.900
In the writer's room, everyone like that, that sort of summer passing, that like blur of being in a month's long post-breakup like depression, everyone in the writer's room had stuff on that.
在编剧室里，每个人都有过那种夏天流逝的感觉，那种长达数月的分手后抑郁的模糊状态，编剧室里的每个人都有过类似的经历。

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:21.460
And I hope this isn't being like sharing too much, but like, you know, Jason Siegel had had a serious relationship when we met him and shot the pilot.
我希望这不算说得太多，但你知道，杰森·席格尔在我们遇见他并拍摄试播集时，刚结束一段认真的感情。

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:25.339
He wrote quite a big movie about it, so I don't think we're spoiling anything.
他为此写了一部很大的电影，所以我认为我们不算剧透。

00:17:25.339 --> 00:17:30.700
But it just to talk about like he was very, that was fresher for him than it was before he wrote that movie.
但我想说的是，对他来说，那段经历比他写那部电影之前还要新鲜。

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:35.980
Maybe he was writing that movie as we're shooting this, but he knew how to lean into that feeling, right?
也许我们在拍这一集的时候他正在写那部电影，但他知道如何沉浸在那样的情绪中，对吧？

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:39.500
He had been in that feeling not too long before we shot this.
在我们拍这一集之前不久，他才刚刚经历过那种感觉。

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.420
You didn't get the feeling like Jason was like, would not draw it.
你不会觉得杰森是在演，他不需要演。

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:46.099
Like Jason knew how to suffer over a relationship ending.
杰森知道如何为一段关系的结束而痛苦。

00:17:46.099 --> 00:17:48.779
That was available to him.
那种情绪对他来说是触手可及的。

00:17:48.779 --> 00:17:49.619
Yes.
是的。

00:17:49.619 --> 00:17:57.420
You know, when Barney comes in, and one of Neil's great things is like his ability to like pivot, like just pivot, like he can go one place.
你知道，当巴尼进来时，尼尔最棒的地方之一就是他那种“转向”的能力，就是那种转向，他可以去往一个地方。

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:01.740
It almost is like slaloming down a hill, and you make it like a sharp turn to clear a gate.
这几乎就像在山上滑雪，你为了绕过旗门而做了一个急转弯。

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.500
Like he's so good at that.
他真的很擅长这个。

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:11.900
And his like real genuine concern for Marshall and then his realization that they're all three single so he thinks at the same time.
还有他那种对马歇尔真诚的关心，然后意识到他们三个现在都单身了，所以他觉得……

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:15.900
And something Neil does in this but I don't know if he did it in season one.
尼尔在这集中做了一些事，我不确定他在第一季有没有做。

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:22.819
I think he did it a little bit but he's really leaning into it when he looks off into the middle distance for these monologues.
我想他做了一点，但他真的在这些独白中看向中远景时，表现得淋漓尽致。

00:18:24.259 --> 00:18:25.779
Yes, he really does that.
是的，他确实做到了。

00:18:25.779 --> 00:18:29.900
And to him in his head, it's like they're swelling strings.
在他脑海里，就像有弦乐在缓缓升起。

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:35.420
Like, this is like a big monologue in a movie that he is delivering.
这就像他在电影里发表的一段宏大的独白。

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:41.819
And you always get the feeling like on the tab over, he thought, he thought up some of these lines.
你总会觉得在剧本之外，他自己想出了一些台词。

00:18:41.819 --> 00:18:44.059
Like, he's so performant.
他太有表演欲了。

00:18:44.059 --> 00:18:46.500
Yeah, Barney's writing his own dialogue for sure.
是的，巴尼肯定在写自己的台词。

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:49.299
And in Barney World, Barney's definitely crafting a dog.
在巴尼的世界里，巴尼绝对是在精心构思。

00:18:49.299 --> 00:18:50.819
I have a fun fact about that speech.
关于那段演讲，我有一个有趣的事实。

00:18:50.819 --> 00:18:52.539
First of all, I mean, Neil fucking crushed that.
首先，我的意思是，尼尔他妈的演得太棒了。

00:18:52.539 --> 00:18:53.380
Amazing.
太神奇了。

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:59.380
And that turn at the end where he realized, And his magical ability to know that Ted and Robin hooked up is great.
最后那个转折，他意识到……还有他那种神奇的能力，能知道泰德和罗宾搞在一起了，这太棒了。

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:00.700
Neil killed that.
尼尔演绝了。

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:09.970
And a fun fact about that speech is it is kind of cribbed from the grapes of wrath, Alex playing.
关于那段演讲的一个有趣事实是，它有点抄袭自《愤怒的葡萄》，亚历克斯·普莱恩。

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:17.109
So we had Fitzgerald in season one, Steinbeck in season two.
所以我们在第一季用了菲茨杰拉德，第二季用了斯坦贝克。

00:19:17.109 --> 00:19:21.430
And it was not, I will not take credit for that at all and I don't think it was Carter either.
而且，我一点也不想把功劳揽在自己身上，我觉得卡特也不是。

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:23.930
Greg Maylands, who was joining the show at that point.
是格雷格·马兰兹，他当时刚加入剧组。

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:34.549
Greg Mayland's a very funny writer, exactly producer, wrote for great dude, wrote for friends for many years, and moved up the ranks to the point where he was running friends for at least a couple of seasons, I think.
格雷格·马兰兹是一位非常有趣的编剧、执行制片人，写过很多好东西，为《老友记》写了很多年，一路晋升，我想他至少有几季是在负责《老友记》的。

00:19:34.549 --> 00:19:37.930
And then we learned he was this huge how much a mother fan and we were huge friends fan.
后来我们得知他是《老爸老妈浪漫史》的超级粉丝，而我们也是《老友记》的超级粉丝。

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:42.289
We hired him to come be kind of like our number two guy on the show in season two.
我们在第二季雇他来担任剧组的二把手。

00:19:42.289 --> 00:19:43.569
He wasn't on season one.
他第一季没在。

00:19:43.569 --> 00:19:46.490
And he came into season two and right away had this great pitch for this Barney speech.
他来到第二季，立刻就为这段巴尼的演讲提出了一个很棒的构思。

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:51.210
We knew we wanted a big Barney speech that ended with Barney that was celebrating them all being single.
我们知道我们需要一段宏大的巴尼演讲，结尾是巴尼在庆祝他们都单身了。

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:57.130
Thank God, we're all single for the first time together and then realizing Robin, like 10 Robin hooked up and deflating.
感谢上帝，我们第一次一起单身了，然后意识到罗宾和泰德搞在一起了，瞬间泄气。

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:01.170
We knew that shape, but we didn't know what the speech was and we're like, it's the opening of this show.
我们知道这个框架，但不知道演讲内容是什么，我们当时想，这是这一集的开场。

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:05.170
The season two, the first sequence, it needs to fucking kill.
第二季的第一个序列，必须得他妈的炸裂。

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:07.970
And Greg said, well, there's that speech in the graves of wrath.
格雷格说，嗯，《愤怒的葡萄》里有那段演讲。

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:09.490
I'm trying to look it up as we're talking.
我正在说话的时候试着查一下。

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:18.049
Where it's like, I'm just like one line from it is like, in the dark, wherever there's a fight for hungry people, a cop beating up a guy or a kid's laughing while hungry, I'll be there.
其中有一句是这样的：在黑暗中，无论哪里有为饥饿者而战的斗争，有警察殴打一个人，或者有孩子在饥饿中欢笑，我都会在那里。

00:20:18.049 --> 00:20:23.210
And we, so Greg, Greg Miland somehow had that speech in his head.
所以格雷格·马兰兹不知怎么就把那段演讲记在脑子里了。

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.369
And he's like, what about that, except it's a Bernie fucking people.
他说，那段怎么样，只不过换成巴尼的版本。

00:20:26.369 --> 00:20:27.210
You know?
你知道吗？

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:30.190
And he was like, yes.
他说，是的。

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:34.509
And like, we looked up the speech from Grapes of Wrath and like, we sort of modeled it on that.
我们查了《愤怒的葡萄》里的演讲，然后我们照着那个模式写了。

00:20:34.509 --> 00:20:37.190
I hadn't thought of that for so many years until I watched this left side.
很多年了我都没想起这件事，直到我看了这一集。

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:39.069
I was like, that was Greg, that was Grapes of Wrath.
我当时想，那是格雷格，那是《愤怒的葡萄》。

00:20:39.069 --> 00:20:44.150
Yeah, you know, this is a little name dropy but it's a fun little thing.
是的，你知道，这有点像是在炫耀人脉，但这是一个有趣的小插曲。

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:46.309
I'm friendly-ish with Jason Rass.
我和杰森·拉斯关系还不错。

00:20:46.309 --> 00:20:47.470
I've known him for a long time.
我认识他很久了。

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:49.950
And he has this great song I love that.
他有一首我很喜欢的歌。

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:52.190
I don't even know if he's released it called Rescue.
我甚至不知道他有没有发行过，叫《救援》。

00:20:52.190 --> 00:21:00.450
And his manager told him like, you should steal from the greats, like steal from like the greatest melodies and composers of all time.
他的经纪人告诉他，你应该向伟人学习，比如向史上最伟大的旋律和作曲家学习。

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:06.410
And so he showed me that this song Rescue is the chord progressions of Pacabel's canon.
所以他向我展示了这首《救援》其实就是帕赫贝尔《卡农》的和弦进行。

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:07.250
Oh yeah.
哦，是的。

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:08.089
But you'd never know it.
但你永远听不出来。

00:21:08.089 --> 00:21:09.609
It's just like he used this kind of map.
他只是用了这种地图。

00:21:09.609 --> 00:21:11.210
He's not the only one that did that.
他不是唯一这么做的人。

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:12.130
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊，是啊。

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.849
But he used the map, right?
但他用了这张地图，对吧？

00:21:13.849 --> 00:21:20.690
And I think like you guys, you can graft something on to a form that really works.
我觉得就像你们一样，你可以把一些东西嫁接到一个真正有效的形式上。

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:21.930
I mean, it's a rhetorical form.
我的意思是，这是一种修辞形式。

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.910
It's almost like a political speech that really is like, yes, we can.
这几乎就像一场政治演讲，真的就像是，“是的，我们可以”。

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:28.349
Yeah, yeah, it's the repeated, it's like a pattern poem.
对，对，就是重复，就像一种模式诗。

00:21:28.349 --> 00:21:33.150
It's like if we take that kind of pattern poem and we kind of do our own person of it, it's there it is.
就像如果我们采用那种模式诗，然后用我们自己的方式演绎出来，就是这样。

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:34.349
It's like weird owl, you know?
就像“怪人奥尔”（Weird Al），你知道吗？

00:21:34.349 --> 00:21:38.630
It's just like, let's take something good and make it our own, and you're weird-alling it.
就像是，让我们拿点好东西，把它变成我们自己的，你就是在“怪人奥尔”化它。

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:48.829
You know what struck me at the end of Barney's speech when he, and then he looks at Ted and Robin and it takes him a second and a half, and he says, Oh, you guys, you guys did it.
你知道巴尼演讲结束时什么打动了我吗？当他看向泰德和罗宾，停顿了一秒半，然后说，哦，你们，你们做到了。

00:21:48.829 --> 00:21:58.190
Like he knows, and I realized there's one other thing and I'll point out in this episode, I think it's with Ted and Marshall, they do have telepathy.
就像他知道一样，我意识到还有一件事，我会在这一集中指出，我认为是泰德和马歇尔，他们确实有心灵感应。

00:21:58.190 --> 00:21:59.029
They do.
他们确实有。

00:21:59.029 --> 00:22:10.470
It's not just in the telepathic moments, like they're getting to know each other so well that they understand, unspoken, they can read each other's body language, they can read stuff between them.
不仅仅是在心灵感应的时刻，他们彼此了解得如此之深，以至于他们能心照不宣，能读懂对方的肢体语言，能读懂他们之间未言明的东西。

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:12.109
And I thought that was fascinating.
我觉得这太迷人了。

00:22:12.109 --> 00:22:18.509
It's a real gesture of the intimacy that is developing between all of these characters.
这是所有这些角色之间正在发展的亲密关系的真实体现。

00:22:18.509 --> 00:22:19.789
They just know things.
他们就是什么都知道。

00:22:19.789 --> 00:22:22.869
I really like, Robin says it's like we have a baby.
我真的很喜欢罗宾说的那句，就像我们有个孩子。

00:22:22.869 --> 00:22:24.190
We had a baby all summer.
我们整个夏天都有个孩子。

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:26.470
We just got together, we have a baby, his name's Marshall.
我们刚在一起，我们就有了个孩子，他叫马歇尔。

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:31.670
And I love that metaphor because Ted is being parental to Marshall.
我喜欢这个比喻，因为泰德在扮演马歇尔的家长。

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:37.990
We're seeing like young Ted who will someday grow up to have these two children parenting Marshall through the summer.
我们看到的是年轻的泰德，他将来会成长为拥有这两个孩子的人，在夏天里像家长一样照顾马歇尔。

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:46.750
And in the end, giving him tough love and being kind of firm and challenging him and using that parenting speed after this summer of comfort.
最后，在经历了舒适的夏天后，对他进行严厉的爱，表现得坚定并挑战他，运用那种育儿节奏。

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:54.549
I mean, it's like when couples get together and they accidentally get pregnant so fast and they barely, like that's what Ted and Bob are like.
我的意思是，就像情侣们在一起后意外怀孕，进展太快，他们几乎……泰德和罗宾就是这样。

00:22:54.549 --> 00:22:58.630
They're like, we just got together, now we have a baby, a screaming, crying baby on our hands.
他们就像是，我们刚在一起，现在我们有个孩子，一个在我们手上尖叫、哭闹的孩子。

00:22:59.829 --> 00:23:02.390
This is teeny tiny, but one joke that I was disappointed.
这虽然是很小的一点，但有一个笑话让我很失望。

00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:07.670
Every now and again, someone will ask me like, do you have regrets when you go back and like change a joke?
时不时会有人问我，当你回看时，你会后悔并想改掉某个笑话吗？

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:13.630
I did have one of those because I misremembered a joke in this and I think my misremembering was funnier.
我确实有一次这种感觉，因为我记错了这一集里的一个笑话，而且我觉得我记错的版本更好笑。

00:23:13.630 --> 00:23:17.029
And maybe we tried to write it this way and we're told to change it or got a note or something.
也许我们试着那样写过，但被告知要改掉，或者收到了什么意见。

00:23:17.029 --> 00:23:21.990
But you know the joke where Marshall comes out and he's talking about how, he has Lily Shampoo.
但你知道马歇尔出来说他有莉莉的洗发水那个笑话吗？

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:28.950
He's talking about how it smells like lavender and seashells and hope, and it's both comforting and erotic at the same time, and then Robin goes, that's mine.
他说那闻起来像薰衣草、贝壳和希望，同时既让人感到安慰又让人感到情欲，然后罗宾说，那是我的。

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.970
I thought we wrote it that Ted goes, that's mine.
我以为我们写的是泰德说，那是我的。

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:34.309
Which is so much funnier.
那才好笑得多。

00:23:34.309 --> 00:23:35.150
It is funnier.
确实更好笑。

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:50.150
I was like, I was heartbroken watching this last night because I've misremembered this for 20 years as the better version of that joke, and I can't remember if we had it that way, and we're like told to chant, I don't know what the hell was happening there, but it's still a kind of a funny joke It would have been so much funnier if it was dead.
我昨晚看的时候心都碎了，因为我把这个笑话的更好版本记错了20年，我不记得我们当时是不是那样写的，或者我们被告知要改掉，我不知道当时发生了什么，但这仍然是一个好笑的笑话，如果换成泰德说的话会好笑得多。

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:53.470
It's also like, I don't know, certainly now.
而且，我不知道，当然现在是这样。

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:57.549
I mean, maybe it wasn't true then, but like who can tell the difference between a female male shampoo at this point?
我的意思是，也许当时不是这样，但现在谁能分清男女洗发水的区别呢？

00:23:57.549 --> 00:23:59.630
Totally, no, it's absolutely.
完全同意，不，绝对是。

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:09.589
I was thinking there must be things in writer's rooms where I thought you guys probably had a really fun time with similarities between the early months of dating and breakups.
我在想编剧室里一定有很多事情，我想你们当时在处理约会初期和分手之间的相似之处时一定玩得很开心。

00:24:09.589 --> 00:24:19.869
Like just like getting an idea and kind of crowdsourcing it and running around, you know, the dry erase board, like getting up all the day and then picking the best ones, right?
就像产生一个想法，然后众包一下，在白板上写写画画，把所有的点子都列出来，然后选出最好的，对吧？

00:24:19.869 --> 00:24:20.869
Yep, absolutely.
是的，绝对是。

00:24:20.869 --> 00:24:24.589
That's what that whole form was where you're going through the summer and to hear the similarities kids.
这就是整个形式，你经历那个夏天，去听那些相似之处。

00:24:24.589 --> 00:24:29.710
And I love that it's Future Ted kind of giving giving the kids this bit of wisdom, how there's overlaps of these things.
我喜欢未来泰德给孩子们传授这种智慧的方式，关于这些事情是如何重叠的。

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:31.470
That was so much fun to pitch on.
那段写起来太有趣了。

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:38.910
Yeah, we found a lot of we found a bunch of good structures in this one, like even that even like mini structures like that, like that's that's a minute of the show right there.
是的，我们在这一集中发现了很多好的结构，甚至像那样的小结构，那可是节目里的一分钟。

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.750
You know, it's 45 seconds of the show is like that concept.
你知道，节目里有45秒就是那个概念。

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:48.181
And then that concept, you know, not too much long past that you're getting into the credit card, mystery of it all.
然后那个概念之后，没过多久你就进入了信用卡那个谜团。

00:24:48.181 --> 00:24:50.509
And again, another mystery episode, right?
又是一个悬疑集，对吧？

00:24:50.509 --> 00:25:02.097
This episode becomes a mystery episode, like so many of the great How Much Mother episodes proving my theory that the mini mysteries within this larger series mystery are so many of our best episodes are that.
这一集变成了一个悬疑集，就像许多伟大的《老爸老妈浪漫史》剧集一样，证明了我的理论，即大型系列谜团中的迷你谜团，是我们许多最棒剧集的特质。

00:25:02.097 --> 00:25:14.349
And I think that's why this episode works, because this is, there's a mystery to solve, and Lily is this completely off screen presence in imaginary land, and this mystery needs to be and I did love that, I love this one.
我认为这就是这一集成功的原因，因为有一个谜团需要解决，而莉莉是一个完全在屏幕外、在想象世界里的存在，这个谜团需要被……我真的很喜欢这一集。

00:25:14.349 --> 00:25:22.269
You know, there's memes that go around like kids, I'm gonna tell you a nine year story that could have taken 15 minutes, right?
你知道，网上流传着这样的梗，孩子们，我要给你们讲一个本可以在15分钟内讲完的九年故事，对吧？

00:25:22.269 --> 00:25:31.990
And I understand that, but it's almost like saying, well, you're gonna die anyway, so you should just get on with it and kill yourself.
我理解这一点，但这就像是说，反正你终究会死，所以你不如直接了当去自杀算了。

