00:42:00.705 --> 00:42:05.901
And in the most satisfying way to learn it, when Lily sees like Marshall, he's having a good night.
以最令人满意的方式去了解，当莉莉看到马歇尔时，他度过了一个愉快的夜晚。

00:42:05.901 --> 00:42:14.456
He's having fun with his friends in the bar and he's laughing and she sees him laughing and she sees him okay and she has no idea the months of agony he's been in.
他和朋友们在酒吧里玩得很开心，他在笑，她看到他在笑，看到他很好，而她根本不知道他经历了几个月的痛苦。

00:42:14.456 --> 00:42:17.080
You know, really smart, really smart storytelling.
你知道，非常聪明，非常聪明的叙事。

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:21.593
Yeah, he seems okay and maybe that means he doesn't need me to walk through this door.
是的，他看起来很好，也许这意味着他不需要我走进那扇门。

00:42:21.593 --> 00:42:24.900
What does it mean that he seems okay and she can't quite do it?
他看起来很好，而她却无法迈出那一步，这意味着什么？

00:42:24.900 --> 00:42:32.477
The end of that episode is one of my favorite moments in season two when you see Lily and you have that great talking about needle drops.
那一集的结尾是我在第二季中最喜欢的时刻之一，当你看到莉莉，还有关于插曲的精彩讨论。

00:42:32.477 --> 00:42:45.365
You talk about the music there, that amazing cover of Boys Don't Cry by Grant Lee Phillips, which was pitched to us by Andy Gowan, our music supervisor, sometimes Carter and I or Josh or somebody would have the idea for a great song.
你谈到了那里的音乐，格兰特·李·菲利普斯翻唱的那首令人惊叹的《男孩不哭》，这是我们的音乐总监安迪·高恩向我们推荐的，有时卡特和我，或者乔什，或者其他人会想到一首好歌。

00:42:45.365 --> 00:42:47.467
This was very much Andy Gowan's pitch.
这很大程度上是安迪·高恩的提议。

00:42:47.467 --> 00:42:53.219
Boys Don't Cry, pushing in on that table, pushing past that table of our gang to finally in that window.
《男孩不哭》，镜头推向那张桌子，推过我们伙伴们的桌子，最后推向那扇窗户。

00:42:53.219 --> 00:42:56.460
She almost comes in, she's not ready, she turns away.
她差点就进来了，但还没准备好，又转身离开了。

00:42:56.460 --> 00:43:01.820
I just remember in the edit room getting chills when we were putting that together and feeling like this is gonna work.
我只记得在剪辑室里，当我们把那段剪辑在一起时，我感到一阵寒意，觉得这一定会成功。

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:03.420
We have a season two.
我们有第二季了。

00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:09.460
There's stuff here, like, right here, you've done one season of TV, you don't know what else is gonna happen past that.
这里有很多内容，毕竟你只做了一季电视剧，你不知道之后还会发生什么。

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:13.500
We were first time show runners that we knew we had stuff there.
我们是第一次担任剧集主管，但我们知道那里有东西可挖。

00:43:13.500 --> 00:43:19.500
We knew we had a condition that would give us six, eight, 10 episodes of that season two and it just felt like rocket fuel there.
我们知道我们有一个前提，可以支撑第二季的六、八、十集，这感觉就像火箭燃料一样。

00:43:19.500 --> 00:43:22.539
I want to see episode two at the end of that episode.
我想在这一集结束时看到第二集。

00:43:22.539 --> 00:43:27.579
It had that cliffhanger-y thing of like, oh shit, how do they get back together here?
它有一种悬念感，让人觉得，天哪，他们怎么才能重新聚在一起？

00:43:27.579 --> 00:43:29.099
How does the gang get back together?
这帮人怎么才能重新聚在一起？

00:43:34.659 --> 00:43:38.739
Well, let me explain another slight change that's happening in season two.
好吧，让我解释一下第二季发生的另一个小变化。

00:43:38.739 --> 00:43:41.619
We had a bunch of bonus episodes for season one.
我们第一季有很多额外剧集。

00:43:41.619 --> 00:43:49.260
We're gonna still do a few perhaps, but in an effort for Craig and I to be able to spend more time with our families.
我们可能还会做一些，但为了让克雷格和我能有更多时间陪伴家人。

00:43:49.260 --> 00:44:00.980
We're going to be doing less bonus episodes, but we're going to try to retain the spirit of those bonus episodes, which were, of course, called general questions.
我们将减少额外剧集的数量，但我们会尽量保持那些额外剧集的精神，当然，它们被称为“一般性问题”。

00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:02.619
General questions?
一般性问题？

00:44:02.619 --> 00:44:04.659
All right, still sucking bath in your eyes.
好吧，还是让洗澡水流进你的眼睛里吧。

00:44:04.659 --> 00:44:05.659
Move through it.
继续进行下去。

00:44:05.659 --> 00:44:06.900
This is where we're gonna have this.
这就是我们要进行这个环节的地方。

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:11.099
The inability to sync this up is not, it's not human error, it's technological.
无法同步不是人为错误，而是技术问题。

00:44:11.099 --> 00:44:17.380
It's just a little, anyway, we are going to do our general questions.
总之，不管怎样，我们要开始我们的“一般性问题”环节了。

00:44:17.380 --> 00:44:18.380
General questions.
一般性问题。

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:19.219
General questions.
一般性问题。

00:44:19.219 --> 00:44:20.820
Alec, I feel like it's your problem.
亚历克，我觉得这是你的问题。

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:22.179
I was like, yes.
我当时想，是的。

00:44:22.179 --> 00:44:26.099
I hate that it leads to finger pointing and divisiveness within the script.
我讨厌它导致剧本内部的指责和分裂。

00:44:26.099 --> 00:44:27.699
That's my regret about it.
这就是我对此感到遗憾的地方。

00:44:27.699 --> 00:44:29.139
We're gonna be doing that right now.
我们现在就要开始做了。

00:44:29.139 --> 00:44:30.500
So Alec, take it away.
所以Alec，开始吧。

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:31.619
What do you have for us?
你为我们准备了什么？

00:44:31.619 --> 00:44:32.619
Absolutely.
当然。

00:44:32.619 --> 00:44:38.420
So you can find us at how we made your mother.com on hit contact.
你可以访问howwemadyourmother.com，点击联系我们。

00:44:38.420 --> 00:44:39.780
You could send in a voice message.
你可以发送语音留言。

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:41.019
You could send in an email.
你可以发送电子邮件。

00:44:41.019 --> 00:44:42.500
You could fill out a form.
你可以填写表格。

00:44:42.500 --> 00:44:43.340
Yes.
是的。

00:44:43.340 --> 00:44:45.099
And also by the way, these can be questions.
顺便说一下，这些可以是问题。

00:44:45.099 --> 00:44:46.619
These can be observations.
也可以是观察心得。

00:44:46.619 --> 00:45:01.059
And what we really like to hear, especially in the voice notes, is tell us what, how I met your mother means to you, how you discovered it, how it changed your life, how, why you keep watching it, who you've, you know, turned onto the show, we wanna hear all those stories, we love them.
我们特别想听到的，尤其是在语音留言中，是告诉我们《老爸老妈浪漫史》对你意味着什么，你是如何发现它的，它如何改变了你的生活，你为什么一直看它，你向谁推荐过这部剧，我们想听听所有这些故事，我们很喜欢它们。

00:45:01.059 --> 00:45:11.300
And, you know, for on Instagram, I mean, I mean, there've been so many years of people posting so many clips and memes and things that sort of kept him alive and thank you to everyone there.
而且，你知道，在Instagram上，我的意思是，多年来人们发布了这么多片段、表情包和内容，这让它保持了活力，感谢那里的每一个人。

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:16.820
And we are, of course, on the social media and how we made your mother.
当然，我们在社交媒体上的账号是howwemadyourmother。

00:45:16.820 --> 00:45:20.940
And on our Instagram is where we solicit your questions.
我们的Instagram账号会征集你们的问题。

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:24.945
Also on the how we made your mother fan club page on Facebook.
Facebook上的“How We Made Your Mother”粉丝俱乐部页面也是如此。

00:45:24.945 --> 00:45:36.380
So from the internet Spencer Mattson says asks What were some of the lessons you learned while filming or writing season one that you knew you wanted to implement in season two?
来自互联网的Spencer Mattson问：在拍摄或编写第一季时，你们学到了哪些想在第二季中实施的经验教训？

00:45:36.619 --> 00:45:44.340
Did you have to do any major pivoting major pivoting this early in the series based on how something in the first season was received?
根据第一季中某些内容的反馈，你们是否需要在剧集早期进行任何重大的调整？

00:45:45.460 --> 00:45:47.589
Well, I touched upon this a little bit.
嗯，我稍微提过这一点。

00:45:47.589 --> 00:45:53.159
We were given we were given a bit of a warning not to be too serious and I think we just didn't do it.
我们确实收到了一些警告，不要太严肃，我想我们只是没有听从。

00:45:53.159 --> 00:45:55.280
We just sort of said yes.
我们只是说好的，我们会考虑一下，但我们知道这部剧的力量在于连载性，以及情感主线一直在延续的事实。

00:45:55.280 --> 00:46:04.079
Okay, we'll take a look at that, but we knew the show's power was serialization and the fact that the emotional throughland kind of kept going.
如果你看这一集，我们会带你走过整个夏天。

00:46:04.079 --> 00:46:07.159
And if you look at this episode, we take you all the way through the summer.
我们带你走过角色们完整的情感旅程。

00:46:07.159 --> 00:46:09.519
We take you all the way through the emotional journey of the characters.
我不认为……我认为最大的教训是不要屈服，不要太担心那些，而是要相信粉丝们会发现它的价值。

00:46:09.519 --> 00:46:18.920
I don't think, I think the biggest lesson was not to cave in and worry too much about that was to think the fans will find this.
我不认为……我认为最大的教训是不要屈服，不要太担心那些，而是要相信粉丝们会发现它的价值。

00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:21.480
This will find people who get it.
这会找到那些懂它的人。

00:46:21.480 --> 00:46:23.280
Thank God, thank you to the fans.
感谢上帝，感谢粉丝们。

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:27.360
Thank you to everyone who's listening to this and who watched the show because we did find you.
感谢每一位收听节目和观看剧集的人，因为我们确实找到了你们。

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:29.280
It's the show we did find you.
这就是我们找到你们的节目。

00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:38.280
And I'm proud to say I think we stayed true to the spirit of what we wanted to do with this show.
我很自豪地说，我认为我们忠于了我们制作这部剧的初衷。

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:42.039
I think if we learned anything, it was like, let's dig deeper into these characters.
我想如果我们学到了什么，那就是：让我们更深入地挖掘这些角色。

00:46:42.039 --> 00:46:45.840
Like we find this other weird trait of Robin in this episode, like along the way.
比如我们在这一集中发现了罗宾身上另一个奇怪的特质，就在这一过程中。

00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:57.159
And we find other, we just kept digging deeper into these characters and these actors, what these actors could do and these relationships and digging deeper into their history and connection.
我们发现了其他特质，我们只是不断地深入挖掘这些角色和这些演员，挖掘这些演员能做到什么，挖掘这些关系，并深入挖掘他们的历史和联系。

00:46:57.159 --> 00:47:07.380
I think that was like, that was definitely something we did more of and wanted to do more of in season two, but we didn't want to mess with kind of the heart and soul of the show, which it is serialized, it just is.
我认为这绝对是我们做得更多、也想在第二季做得更多的事情，但我们不想破坏这部剧的核心和灵魂，它就是一部连续剧，事实就是如此。

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:13.320
Josh, was there anything as an actor who now, okay, you're in season two.
乔什，作为一名演员，现在好了，你进入了第二季，有什么感觉吗？

00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:17.639
There's a little, maybe a little more of a, you could breathe out, you're actually doing this.
是不是稍微，也许稍微多了一点，你可以松一口气，你真的做到了。

00:47:17.639 --> 00:47:23.679
is staying on TV for a while, and with that aside, you can lean in a little more.
能在电视上播一段时间，抛开这一点不谈，你可以更投入一些。

00:47:23.679 --> 00:47:26.880
Was there a pressure valve released a little bit for you?
对你来说，压力阀是否稍微释放了一些？

00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:28.159
Or was it the opposite?
还是恰恰相反？

00:47:28.159 --> 00:47:30.280
It was more doing this now.
现在做这件事更多了。

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:31.880
Is it more pressure?
压力更大了吗？

00:47:31.880 --> 00:47:40.760
No, I think it was less in as much as I knew they were not gonna replace me with another guy to play the role.
不，我认为压力反而小了，因为我知道他们不会找另一个人来代替我演这个角色。

00:47:40.760 --> 00:47:43.679
I felt like some measure of job security.
我感到了一种工作保障。

00:47:43.679 --> 00:47:48.000
I mean, we were still, you know, we were still season to season.
我的意思是，我们仍然，你知道，我们仍然是一季一季地拍。

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:50.000
We weren't, we weren't getting two season pickups.
我们并没有，我们并没有获得两季的预订。

00:47:50.000 --> 00:47:51.000
We were very dependent.
我们非常依赖收视。

00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:54.199
We didn't even know if we'd get a back nine in season two at this point, right?
当时我们甚至不知道第二季是否能获得后九集的预订，对吧？

00:47:54.199 --> 00:47:55.639
Like we knew we had 13.
我们只知道我们有13集。

00:47:55.639 --> 00:47:56.480
Correct.
没错。

00:47:56.480 --> 00:47:57.679
Are they gonna air all 13?
他们会播完这13集吗？

00:47:57.679 --> 00:48:02.000
You got it, you know, you got to stay in the zone where they're still feeling good about it.
你懂的，你必须保持在他们仍然对节目感觉良好的状态。

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:07.000
I think that I felt really good about where Ted was.
我想我对泰德所处的位置感觉非常好。

00:48:07.239 --> 00:48:19.727
I mean, it's certainly like, even though it's an, you know, you're an actor, It's more fun to play the wiser, steadier character in terms of, I mean, it can be fun to play despair and all that stuff.
我的意思是，这当然，即使你是一名演员，扮演更睿智、更稳重的角色会更有趣，我的意思是，扮演绝望之类的角色也很有趣。

00:48:19.727 --> 00:48:27.634
But I liked that Ted didn't have to be, at least in this episode, the person who was punched in the face or left at the altar.
但我喜欢泰德不必，至少在这一集中，成为那个被打脸或在婚礼上被抛弃的人。

00:48:27.634 --> 00:48:31.399
He got to really get the girl, he got to be the good friend.
他真的追到了女孩，他成为了好朋友。

00:48:31.399 --> 00:48:41.440
I thought Ted's virtues were really on display in this episode, And I think that's why I liked it so much was because I didn't have to open up a vein, you know.
我认为泰德的优点在这一集中得到了充分展现，我想这就是我如此喜欢它的原因，因为我不必“剖开血管”去表演，你知道的。

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:43.697
Yeah, I think that's another thing we learned.
是的，我想这是我们学到的另一件事。

00:48:43.697 --> 00:48:45.119
We put the focus on Marshall.
我们将焦点放在了马歇尔身上。

00:48:45.119 --> 00:48:49.989
Marshall is the focus of the romantic quandary in this episode and in this opening arc of season two.
马歇尔是这一集以及第二季开篇故事中浪漫困境的焦点。

00:48:49.989 --> 00:48:51.869
And that was something we wanted to do.
这是我们想做的事情。

00:48:51.869 --> 00:48:54.907
We said it was the spotlight was on Ted all through season one.
我们说过，第一季的聚光灯一直都在泰德身上。

00:48:54.907 --> 00:49:00.114
Let's get Ted with Robin and let's take a look at the pain and struggles of somebody else here for a minute.
让我们让泰德和罗宾在一起，让我们花点时间看看其他人的痛苦和挣扎。

00:49:00.114 --> 00:49:01.175
And that was exciting.
这很令人兴奋。

00:49:01.175 --> 00:49:03.200
It was exciting because it was a new move.
这很令人兴奋，因为这是一个新的举动。

00:49:03.199 --> 00:49:06.950
and we couldn't do that when Marshall and Lily were just super couple.
当马歇尔和莉莉还是超级情侣时，我们做不到这一点。

00:49:06.950 --> 00:49:09.039
We needed to shake them up a bit there.
我们需要稍微动摇一下他们。

00:49:09.039 --> 00:49:13.039
At Spratty asks, Josh looks more tan in the season premiere.
At Spratty问，乔什在第二季首播中看起来更黑了。

00:49:13.039 --> 00:49:14.320
Had you spent your time away?
你这段时间去哪儿了？

00:49:15.920 --> 00:49:17.771
You know, it's so funny.
你知道，这太有趣了。

00:49:17.771 --> 00:49:27.723
I have very vivid memories of my second season hiatus, but the first season hiatus in between one and two, oh, I know what I was.
我对第二季的间歇期记忆犹新，但第一季和第二季之间的间歇期，哦，我知道我在干什么。

00:49:27.723 --> 00:49:39.681
I believe I did a play in Poughkeepsie at the New York stage in film, Up at Vassar, which is a theater that I have worked with since I was like in college.
我相信我在波基普西的纽约舞台电影节上演了一部戏，在瓦萨学院，那是我从大学起就一直合作的剧院。

00:49:39.681 --> 00:49:43.307
I'm almost sure I did a play there that summer.
我几乎确定那个夏天我在那里演了一部戏。

00:49:43.307 --> 00:49:50.559
So I was just, you know, I was in Upstate New York doing theater and going to the tanning bed.
所以我只是，你知道，我在纽约州北部演戏，然后去晒黑床。

00:49:52.559 --> 00:49:53.723
Throughout the summer.
整个夏天都在晒。

00:49:53.723 --> 00:50:00.119
What's funny is you're tanning, you got very tan in the half hour after after you came in from the rain and on the stoop.
有趣的是，你晒黑了，在你从雨中走进来并站在门廊上后的半小时里，你变得非常黑。

00:50:00.119 --> 00:50:02.559
That's the weird thing about needing to play that continuity.
这就是需要保持那种连续性带来的奇怪之处。

00:50:02.559 --> 00:50:04.880
It's like, everyone looks a little different.
就像是，每个人看起来都有点不一样。

00:50:04.880 --> 00:50:06.480
The apartment looks a little different.
公寓看起来也有点不一样。

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:09.639
I have, people often say that I look tan.
我经常听人说我看起来晒黑了。

00:50:09.639 --> 00:50:13.519
I think that if I get a little bit of sun, I kind of brown up.
我觉得如果我晒一点太阳，皮肤就会变棕。

00:50:13.519 --> 00:50:15.519
You know, I don't burn very easily.
你知道，我不太容易晒伤。

00:50:15.519 --> 00:50:17.159
And it's always a little shocking to people.
而且这总是让人们感到有点惊讶。

00:50:17.159 --> 00:50:18.079
Boy, are we different.
天哪，我们真是太不一样了。

00:50:18.079 --> 00:50:22.719
Well, yes, this just shows that two different species can be friends, Craig.
嗯，是的，这正好说明了两个不同的物种可以成为朋友，克雷格。

00:50:22.719 --> 00:50:26.199
The iris and the Jews don't have shared the overlap there.
爱尔兰人和犹太人之间并没有什么共同点。

00:50:27.699 --> 00:50:36.860
At Ryan Schwartz TV says, it's supposed to be the same day, but the brown couch from season one has been swapped for the red couch in the season two premiere.
Ryan Schwartz TV 说，这应该是同一天发生的事，但第一季里的棕色沙发在第二季首播时被换成了红色沙发。

00:50:36.860 --> 00:50:38.500
Why the change?
为什么要换？

00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:40.679
Okay, I'll tell you.
好吧，我告诉你。

00:50:40.679 --> 00:50:45.059
One, we got a bunch of notes at the end of season one and a bunch of some of them we agreed with.
第一，我们在第一季结束时收到了一堆反馈，其中一些我们是认同的。

00:50:45.059 --> 00:50:46.860
We didn't want to un-serialize the show.
我们不想让这部剧失去连贯性。

00:50:46.860 --> 00:50:49.340
We wanted to keep it that way, but we did get it.
我们想保持那种连贯，但我们确实收到了反馈。

00:50:49.340 --> 00:50:50.860
I think we got a note from the network.
我想我们收到了来自电视网的反馈。

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:54.659
It may have even been from Pam Freiman too, or everybody just sort of agreed.
甚至可能是帕姆·弗莱曼（Pam Freiman）提的，或者大家只是达成了一致。

00:50:54.659 --> 00:50:58.420
Season one, visually, that apartment looks a little dark.
从视觉上看，第一季那间公寓看起来有点暗。

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:08.619
And I think we, Carter and I wanted it to be like, not the fancy, shiny, like the friend's apartment, huge, and this sort of weird, like, that purpley kind of like, lots of like almost neon colored things.
我想卡特和我当时想让它看起来不像那种华丽、闪亮、《老友记》里那种巨大的公寓，而是那种奇怪的、紫色的、有很多近乎霓虹色装饰的风格。

00:51:08.619 --> 00:51:12.219
We wanted it to be more like, this is a New York apartment, it's still too big.
我们想让它更像是一间纽约公寓，虽然还是太大了。

00:51:12.219 --> 00:51:14.900
But we wanted it to be a plausible New York apartment.
但我们想让它看起来像是一间合理的纽约公寓。

00:51:14.900 --> 00:51:19.860
I think we probably steered our set designer and his team towards like, let's grid it up a little bit.
我想我们可能引导我们的布景设计师和他的团队往“稍微规整一点”的方向调整。

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:21.019
It doesn't need to look shiny.
它不需要看起来闪闪发光。

00:51:21.019 --> 00:51:22.579
It doesn't need to look neon.
它不需要看起来像霓虹灯。

00:51:22.579 --> 00:51:25.780
And I think we might have gone a few clicks too far.
我觉得我们可能做得有点过头了。

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:27.300
Season one does look a little dark.
第一季看起来确实有点暗。

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:28.260
The couch is a little dark.
沙发有点暗。

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:29.420
The walls a little dark.
墙壁也有点暗。

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:32.219
We decided to just brighten everything up.
我们决定把一切都调亮。

00:51:32.219 --> 00:51:33.059
And we did it.
我们确实这么做了。

00:51:33.059 --> 00:51:33.900
Hoping no one would notice.
希望没人会注意到。

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:39.739
But of course, in the interim, people are like, why the fuck is there a different couch from when they left the apartment to the night before?
但当然，在此期间，人们会问，为什么他们离开公寓到前一天晚上之间，沙发就换了？

00:51:39.739 --> 00:51:44.420
It's also like you were depending on four months of amnesia for people.
这就像你指望观众有四个月的失忆症一样。

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:45.260
Right.
没错。

00:51:45.260 --> 00:51:46.099
It wasn't on streaming.
当时又没有流媒体。

00:51:46.099 --> 00:51:48.579
You hadn't seen how much a mother since May.
你从五月开始就没看过《老爸老妈浪漫史》了。

00:51:48.579 --> 00:51:49.420
No, it's September.
不，那是九月。

00:51:49.420 --> 00:51:56.500
People literally go from episode 22 of season one right into episode and you're like, why is the couch different?
人们确实是从第一季第22集直接看到下一集，然后你会想，为什么沙发变了？

00:51:56.500 --> 00:51:58.739
Why is the couch a different bitch?
为什么沙发换了个样？

00:51:58.739 --> 00:51:59.739
Yeah.
是啊。

00:51:59.739 --> 00:52:02.019
We've heard this a lot and we will continue to hear it.
我们听过很多次这种问题，以后还会继续听到。

00:52:02.019 --> 00:52:03.019
It's a great question.
这是一个很好的问题。

00:52:03.019 --> 00:52:04.019
It is a great question.
确实是个好问题。

00:52:04.019 --> 00:52:05.219
I never knew that though.
不过我从来都不知道。

00:52:05.219 --> 00:52:10.340
It was my goddamn fake apartment and I didn't know it was a new couch.
那可是我该死的假公寓，我竟然不知道换了新沙发。

00:52:10.340 --> 00:52:13.659
All right, here's a little BSL.
好了，这里有一点 BSL。

00:52:13.659 --> 00:52:14.659
BSL.
BSL。
BSL。BSL。

00:52:14.659 --> 00:52:16.659
What does that word come from?
这个词是从哪来的？

00:52:16.659 --> 00:52:20.340
I think it's BTS mixed with ASL, which I'm always thinking about.
我想是 BTS（幕后）和 ASL（美国手语）的混合，我一直在想这个。

00:52:20.340 --> 00:52:23.300
Sorry, BTS though, it'll be behind sign language.
抱歉，BTS 应该是幕后（Behind The Scenes）。

00:52:23.300 --> 00:52:26.579
My goodness, a little behind the scenes question from at keenin99.
天哪，来自 @keenin99 的一个小小的幕后问题。

00:52:26.579 --> 00:52:26.900
Okay.
好的。

00:52:27.860 --> 00:52:32.659
Do the actors in the background of bar and restaurant scenes actually have conversations?
酒吧和餐厅场景背景里的演员真的在对话吗？

00:52:32.659 --> 00:52:36.099
It seems like they mouth real words, but have silent conversations.
看起来他们是在做出口型，但实际上是静音对话。

00:52:36.099 --> 00:52:37.380
How does that work?
这是怎么运作的？

00:52:37.380 --> 00:52:38.900
Literally what you just said.
字面意思就是你刚才说的。

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:40.340
That's what it is.
就是这样。

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:43.300
Your question answered itself.
你的问题已经自问自答了。

00:52:43.300 --> 00:52:44.340
And yes, it's weird.
而且是的，这很奇怪。

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:48.260
It's generally the second AD who kind of is in charge of the background.
通常是第二副导演负责背景演员。

00:52:48.260 --> 00:52:49.300
Is it the second AD?
是第二副导演吗？

00:52:49.300 --> 00:52:50.420
Generally the second AD.
通常是第二副导演。

00:52:50.420 --> 00:52:51.940
Extra points, generally.
通常是临时演员。

00:52:53.139 --> 00:52:55.139
It was Chris, right, who was our second.
是克里斯（Chris）吧，我们的第二副导演。

00:52:56.099 --> 00:52:57.219
Maybe it was the second second.
也许是第二副导演的助理。

00:52:57.219 --> 00:53:07.539
I can't remember, there's a second second assistant director, but they're ordered sometimes quite harshly, like, we can hear your whispering, like they really have to mouth conversations.
我不记得了，有第二副导演，但他们有时会被严厉地要求，比如“我们能听到你们的窃窃私语”，所以他们真的必须做出口型。

00:53:07.539 --> 00:53:08.739
They really have to mime it.
他们真的必须模仿对话。

00:53:08.739 --> 00:53:16.659
It must be the most awkward job in the world to show up and sit with a stranger you were just paired with pretending to talk silently to each other.
这一定是世界上最尴尬的工作了，到现场和一个刚认识的陌生人坐在一起，假装在进行无声的交谈。

00:53:16.659 --> 00:53:24.845
But there were a couple people that, some of them were like UCLA students or like people that would show up like through the years that were like bar regulars.
但有几个人，比如一些加州大学洛杉矶分校的学生，或者多年来一直出现的人，他们就像是酒吧常客。

00:53:24.845 --> 00:53:39.825
I'm thinking of a couple, there were a couple women that were just like really cool and they were always kind of in the bar like like maybe half three quarters of the seasons and we got to know them because you're sitting around eating checks mix and drinking fake beer and they were lovely.
我想到几位女士，她们真的很酷，她们几乎在酒吧里待了三季之久，我们和她们很熟，因为大家坐在一起吃零食、喝假啤酒，她们很可爱。

00:53:39.825 --> 00:53:44.767
So shout out to the background actors, I always thought this job is actually harder than my job.
所以向背景演员致敬，我一直觉得这份工作其实比我的工作更难。

00:53:44.767 --> 00:53:46.260
This is a very difficult job.
这是一份非常困难的工作。

00:53:46.260 --> 00:53:50.980
It's not an easy job, and at one point Conan O'Brien did that job on our show.
这不是一份轻松的工作，有一次柯南·奥布莱恩（Conan O'Brien）还在我们剧里干过这活。

00:53:50.980 --> 00:53:52.823
Yeah, we'll get to that.
是的，我们稍后会讲到。

00:53:52.823 --> 00:54:04.420
And finally, a bigger question to end with here is, at Camore, at Camore says, how much of season two was planned when you ended season one as you did?
最后，以一个更大的问题结束，@Camore 问，当你们结束第一季时，第二季有多少内容是提前规划好的？

00:54:04.420 --> 00:54:06.582
Was it a vague outline, or was it more?
是一个模糊的大纲，还是更多？

00:54:06.582 --> 00:54:07.858
We've set ourselves up.
我们为自己设定了局面。

00:54:07.858 --> 00:54:09.300
Let's see what comes next.
看看接下来会发生什么。

00:54:09.300 --> 00:54:40.019
It was more than that it wasn't let set ourselves up and see what comes next but certainly wasn't meticulously plotted out i think you're looking for conditions to play what can give us some rocket fuel for a bunch of episodes marshall molly breakup ten robin get together that's a condition we can play we can get and then how do we have fun and be really interesting within that how do we find the twists and turns of that but if you set up that condition you're just kind of getting the raw material kind of like the fuel in the rocket and then navigating where that fuel takes you is the next step.
不仅仅是“设定局面看看接下来会发生什么”，但也绝对没有经过精密的策划。我认为你在寻找可以发挥的条件，为后续剧集提供动力，比如马歇尔和莉莉分手，泰德和罗宾在一起，这是我们可以利用的条件，然后我们思考如何在其中玩得开心、变得有趣，如何找到其中的转折点。但如果你设定了那个条件，你只是在获取原材料，就像火箭里的燃料，然后导航燃料带你去哪里就是下一步。

00:54:40.019 --> 00:54:49.659
And we probably, you know, over the summer, as we were starting to break and write those scripts, we got more like plotting the navigation points on that, on that journey.
我们可能在夏天开始拆解和编写剧本时，在旅程中规划了更多的导航点。

00:54:49.659 --> 00:54:59.780
But we knew, I think we gave ourselves a gift at the end of season one, even though season one ends sad and bittersweet, it was a gift to our future selves because we had a story to tell.
但我们知道，我想我们在第一季结尾给了自己一份礼物，尽管第一季的结局是悲伤且苦乐参半的，但这对未来的我们来说是一份礼物，因为我们有故事要讲。

00:54:59.780 --> 00:55:02.219
We had to dig our way out of that problem.
我们必须想办法摆脱那个困境。

00:55:02.219 --> 00:55:03.260
Can I ask you, Craig?
我可以问你吗，克雷格？

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:08.420
So by the time we come back, like August, I don't remember mid-August, let's say, early August, mid-August.
所以当我们回来的时候，比如八月中旬，假设是八月初或八月中旬。

00:55:08.420 --> 00:55:11.480
You guys were handed episode one.
你们拿到了第一集。

00:55:11.480 --> 00:55:13.860
How many episodes deep are you into the writing?
你们写到第几集了？

00:55:13.860 --> 00:55:15.500
How many episodes have you broken?
你们拆解了多少集？

00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:16.980
Like, where are you at that point?
那时候你们进度如何？

00:55:16.980 --> 00:55:23.099
When you show up to that first, when the actors show up to that first table read in whatever was late August, or whatever it would be, if I remember.
当演员们出现在八月底的第一次剧本围读会时，如果我没记错的话。

00:55:23.099 --> 00:55:23.920
I think it was a little early.
我想那是稍微早一点。

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:25.579
I was always like mid-August maybe, yeah.
我记得大概是八月中旬吧，是的。

00:55:25.579 --> 00:55:27.420
Mid-August, early August.
八月中旬，八月初。

00:55:27.420 --> 00:55:38.260
You, we would hope to be at like six scripts maybe, five or six scripts written or degrees of written, and then the outline and the shape for a few past that.
我们希望已经写好了大约六个剧本，或者处于不同写作阶段的剧本，然后后面几集有大纲和雏形。

00:55:38.260 --> 00:55:43.539
And then it just slowly, I was thought of it as like, the season of the TV is like Pac-Man eating these pellets.
然后它就慢慢地，我一直觉得电视季就像吃豆人吃豆子一样。

00:55:43.539 --> 00:55:45.860
Okay, that episode got written and shot, that's a pellet.
好了，那一集写完并拍完了，那是一颗豆子。

00:55:45.860 --> 00:55:47.139
And it's just coming, coming, coming.
它就这样不断地来。

00:55:47.139 --> 00:55:49.340
There's this thing got to bite, eating them up.
这东西必须得吃掉它们。

00:55:49.340 --> 00:55:51.900
And you're trying to stay as far ahead of that as you can.
你试图尽可能地保持领先。

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:54.619
I realize I'm making us the ghosts in Pac-Man or something.
我意识到我把自己比作吃豆人里的幽灵了。

00:55:54.619 --> 00:55:56.380
I don't know, the metaphor is falling apart.
不知道，这个比喻有点站不住脚了。

00:55:56.380 --> 00:56:04.380
But yeah, we would always, you'd wanna have six scripts and a couple outlines or else you'd feel really screwed.
不过确实，我们总是希望手里有六个剧本和几个大纲，否则你会觉得非常糟糕。

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:11.219
But by the time we show up, that's the most comfortable you as the show runner is going to feel because you're the most ahead of it you'll ever be.
但当我们开始拍摄时，作为剧集主管，那已经是你感觉最轻松的时候了，因为那是你进度最领先的时候。

00:56:11.219 --> 00:56:14.019
Like, you're never going to be six episodes ahead the rest of the season.
因为你永远不可能领先整个季度六集。

00:56:14.019 --> 00:56:17.019
No, you're going to just watch that catch up to you.
不，你只会眼睁睁看着进度追上你。

00:56:17.019 --> 00:56:21.460
Watch the devouring Pac-Man Monster catch up and eat up those episodes.
看着那个贪婪的吃豆人怪物追上来，把那些剧集吞噬掉。

00:56:21.460 --> 00:56:25.900
And yeah, it's invigorated, as you always say.
是的，正如你常说的，这让人精神振奋。

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:28.739
It is a marathon run at a sprint space.
这是一场以冲刺速度进行的马拉松。

00:56:28.739 --> 00:56:29.739
Yeah.
没错。

00:56:29.739 --> 00:56:30.500
24 episodes.
24集。

00:56:30.500 --> 00:56:36.739
I think we did 22 in season two, and when we became more successful, we started getting 24, but 22 is plenty.
我想我们在第二季做了22集，当我们变得更成功后，我们开始做24集，但22集已经足够了。

00:56:38.179 --> 00:56:47.420
So we like to end a lot of these episodes with a letter that's been sent in to how we made your mother.com to contact.
我们喜欢在很多集结尾读一封发给howwemadyourmother.com联系页面的信。

00:56:48.340 --> 00:56:53.539
And we got a delightful, wonderful letter from Sophie in Germany.
我们收到了一封来自德国苏菲的令人愉悦、精彩的信。

00:56:53.539 --> 00:57:04.780
And again, if you want to send one in, we even if it doesn't make it on the air, which it might please let us know how what the show means to you and anything else you want to share with us about.
再说一次，如果你想寄信过来，即使没能在节目中播出（虽然有可能播出），也请告诉我们这部剧对你意味着什么，以及任何你想和我们分享的事情。

00:57:04.780 --> 00:57:05.575
We love it.
我们很喜欢。

00:57:05.575 --> 00:57:06.514
It's so nice.
太好了。

00:57:06.514 --> 00:57:07.526
It's so great.
太棒了。

00:57:07.526 --> 00:57:08.026
Yeah.
是的。

00:57:08.026 --> 00:57:10.918
So this one says, hi, my name is Sophie.
这封信写道：嗨，我叫苏菲。

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:13.391
I am 15 years old and I live in Germany.
我今年15岁，住在德国。

00:57:13.391 --> 00:57:17.503
I discovered how I met your mother pretty precisely a year ago.
我发现《老爸老妈浪漫史》大概是在一年前。

00:57:17.503 --> 00:57:21.420
I was alone on a nine-hour train ride back home from Berlin.
当时我独自一人坐着九小时的火车从柏林回家。

00:57:21.420 --> 00:57:26.139
I logged into the train Wi-Fi and a website popped up with hundreds of movies and series.
我连上了火车的Wi-Fi，弹出了一个有数百部电影和剧集的网站。

00:57:26.139 --> 00:57:33.579
For some reason, I decided to click on how I met your mother, not knowing how much I would fall in love with this show and how it would change me.
出于某种原因，我决定点击《老爸老妈浪漫史》，不知道我会多么爱上这部剧，也不知道它会如何改变我。

00:57:33.579 --> 00:57:36.365
I am someone who worries a lot about their future.
我是一个非常担心未来的人。

00:57:36.365 --> 00:57:44.275
I feel like I am missing out on all my teenage experiences not having kissed anyone yet or feeling like anyone will ever fall in love with me.
我觉得我错过了所有的青春体验，还没吻过任何人，也觉得不会有人爱上我。

00:57:44.275 --> 00:57:54.747
Every day I think about where I will be in 10 years, what job will I be doing, who I will still be with and who I am going to lose and if anyone is ever going to love me the way Ted loves.
每天我都在想10年后我会身在何处，我会做什么工作，我会和谁在一起，我会失去谁，以及是否有人会像泰德爱人那样爱我。

00:57:54.747 --> 00:58:04.440
Saying that makes me feel guilty of thinking so much about myself knowing what what is happening to the world in politics, climate change, AI, social media, the list goes on.
说这些让我感到内疚，因为我知道世界上正在发生政治、气候变化、人工智能、社交媒体等各种事情，我却想这么多关于自己的事。

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:08.960
I often feel like the whole world is just going under and that's just what my future will be like.
我经常觉得整个世界都要完了，而那也就是我的未来。

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:18.960
All of these thoughts have taken up a lot of space the last four years, but since I met Ted Marshall, Lily, Robin, and Barney, the hope of everything eventually working out has been fighting the darker thoughts.
过去四年里，所有这些想法占据了我很多空间，但自从我遇见了泰德、马歇尔、莉莉、罗宾和巴尼，一切终将好转的希望一直在与黑暗的想法作斗争。

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:22.079
How I met your mother comforts me in a way nothing else does.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》以一种其他事物无法比拟的方式安慰了我。

00:58:22.079 --> 00:58:29.739
It taught me how important friendships are, but I have to let things change and that every phase of your life is important and will lead you to something.
它教会了我友谊有多重要，我必须接受事物的变化，人生的每一个阶段都很重要，都会引导你走向某些东西。

00:58:29.739 --> 00:58:31.460
So thank you, Josh and Craig.
所以谢谢你们，乔什和克雷格。

00:58:31.460 --> 00:58:38.989
Thank you so much for creating something that brings me hope, that makes me laugh and cry, and that will always have a special place in my heart.
非常感谢你们创造了带给我希望、让我又哭又笑，并且永远在我心中占据特殊位置的作品。

00:58:38.989 --> 00:58:39.829
That's beautiful.
太美了。

00:58:39.829 --> 00:58:45.269
My God, Sophie, you were the wisest 15-year-old, I think I ever met.
天哪，苏菲，你是我见过最睿智的15岁孩子。

00:58:45.269 --> 00:58:46.349
Yeah.
是的。

00:58:46.349 --> 00:58:48.750
We're really honored the show has meant that to you.
我们真的很荣幸这部剧对你有这样的意义。

00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:51.389
And yeah, thank you for sharing that with us.
是的，谢谢你和我们分享这些。

00:58:51.389 --> 00:58:59.909
Yeah, Sophie, I just want to say, I mean, you sound like you have an enormous heart, And I often find that about people that love how I met your mother.
是的，苏菲，我只想说，你听起来有一颗宽广的心，我经常发现喜欢《老爸老妈浪漫史》的人都是这样。

00:58:59.909 --> 00:59:03.630
They're just very deeply feeling people.
他们是非常感性的人。

00:59:03.630 --> 00:59:12.469
And I think that as you demonstrate in your letter, you can hold many things in your awareness at one given, any given time.
正如你在信中所展示的那样，我认为你可以在任何特定时刻同时关注很多事情。

00:59:12.469 --> 00:59:25.856
You can hold the pain and uncertainty of the world, the collective, what's going on in the larger kind of landscape, as well as your own thoughts and feelings hurts and yearnings and we can hold all of that.
你可以同时承载世界的痛苦和不确定性、集体层面的大环境，以及你自己的想法、感受、伤痛和渴望，我们可以包容这一切。

00:59:25.856 --> 00:59:46.550
I'm reminded of something that I think it's in, one of Elizabeth Gilbert's books or talks she talks about in a refugee camp in some war-torn country and it was just a terrible situation and this person had gotten to know some of the young kids in this refugee camp and they were all talking about who has a crush on who.
我想起伊丽莎白·吉尔伯特的一本书或演讲中提到的事，她谈到在某个饱受战争蹂躏国家的难民营里，情况非常糟糕，但那个人认识了难民营里的一些小孩子，他们还在谈论谁暗恋谁。

00:59:47.989 --> 01:00:01.438
And they were still, even in this dire humanitarian situation, they were still wanted love and they still wanted to know who liked them and did they like, you know, who they liked and that was still of interest to me, it's them.
即使在那种严峻的人道主义困境中，他们依然渴望爱，依然想知道谁喜欢他们，他们喜欢谁，这对我来说依然很有意义。

01:00:01.438 --> 01:00:10.109
So I don't think that worrying about the world and being concerned about your own future and will you find someone to love and someone to love you.
所以我认为担心世界和关心自己的未来，以及是否会找到爱人，这些并不矛盾。

01:00:10.109 --> 01:00:12.882
And I don't think those are mutually exclusive.
我不认为这些是互斥的。

01:00:12.882 --> 01:00:15.536
I think that humans, we can hold all of that.
我认为人类可以同时承载这一切。

01:00:15.536 --> 01:00:26.389
And even though how I met your mother was not a directly political show, I think there is something political about being a good friend and being showing up for people and being loyal.
尽管《老爸老妈浪漫史》不是一部直接的政治剧，但我认为做一个好朋友、支持他人并保持忠诚，本身就具有某种政治意义。

01:00:26.389 --> 01:00:36.309
And those things are needed in our society, you know, Ted says, love is the best thing we do, you know, it's somewhat a political statement on some level if you want to get granular about it.
这些在我们的社会中是必需的，你知道，泰德说“爱是我们所做的最好的事”，如果你想深究的话，这在某种程度上是一个政治声明。

01:00:36.309 --> 01:00:39.230
So I think we can do all those things.
所以我认为我们可以同时做到这些。

01:00:39.230 --> 01:00:42.340
I don't think you have to feel bad about any of that.
我不认为你必须为此感到难过。

01:00:42.340 --> 01:00:43.900
I am guilty.
我有罪。

01:00:43.900 --> 01:00:45.980
Please equip me.
请赦免我。

01:00:45.980 --> 01:00:51.179
All sins are forgiven in New York City."
在纽约市，所有的罪过都会被原谅。

